ever since russia invaded ukraine much  of the western world has bombarded them  with restrictions boycotts and sanctions  for example the biggest companies in the  world including apple amazon adobe  netflix and disney have all stopped  operations in russia  similarly many countries have stopped  importing russian oil and some countries  won't even let russian aircraft fly over  them not to mention most russian banks  and accounts have been completely frozen  the idea was that we could help ukraine  by hurting russia economically and that  an economic crash would cause russia to  back out faster but so far this doesn't  seem to be the case the russian economy  is indeed hurting but the russian ruble  is actually stronger than ever and if  you don't believe me just take a look at  the charts  initially the ruble did crash 50 as soon  as the sanctions were implemented but  since then the rule ball has skyrocketed  150 percent this means that the ruble is  currently the strongest it's been in  seven years now you might be thinking  that this must be because the dollar is  performing even worse or something like  that but this isn't the case either in  fact the dollar is the strongest it's  been in 20 years on a relative basis and  this just makes it even more significant  that the russian rupaul is skyrocketing  against the dollar  so what in the world is happening with  the russian ruble and why is it mooning  despite the sanctions  one of the top reasons that the ruble is  doing so well is because many of the  sanctions imposed on russia aren't even  that effective especially the energy  sanctions you see the european union is  way more dependent on russian energy  than they would like to admit and though  i'm sure many of them would like to ban  russian imports completely this is  simply not realistic here's the thing  russia is the world's largest gas  exporter and the second largest oil  exporter so it's not really possible to  completely cut off russian exports  overnight if they did do this it would  be the countries that are importing oil  that would collapse first but wait a  minute didn't the eu implement a russian  oil embargo  well the answer is yes but there's a lot  of fine print and exceptions  first of all the goal is to ban most  imports by the end of the year not by  tomorrow also certain countries like  slovenia and hungary are partially or  even completely exempt from the embargo  such countries are not only extremely  dependent on russian exports but more  importantly their landlord meaning that  they can't receive oil through the sea  these countries primarily get their oil  through pipelines directly from russia  and as you would guess rebuilding such  infrastructure would take several years  if not well over a decade and given that  most western countries are already  headed towards the recession completely  operating their crude oil source is the  last thing these countries want to do  and given that oil prices are  skyrocketing as well energy exports have  just become even more profitable for  russia in fact during their first  hundred days of the war it's estimated  that russia pulled in 60 billion worth  of revenue from fossil fuel exports to  the eu alone also let's not forget that  not every country is against russia most  of us are constantly exposed to western  media so it seems like the entire world  is against russia but this isn't true by  any means many countries actually  support russia including belarus cuba  nicaragua venezuela and kyrgyzstan but  you don't even have to support russia to  help them if you simply stay neutral and  continue importing russian oil that  itself is a massive benefit for russia  and this is exactly what many countries  have been doing particularly china and  india these countries have no financial  reason to stop importing russian energy  in fact they're incentivized to import  more  you see it as western countries slowly  step away from russian energy the price  of russian energy decreases and this  gives countries like china and india the  perfect opportunity to buy for a  discount and that's exactly what they've  been doing china has risen to be  russia's top all-importer having bought  12.6 billion worth of oil since the war  started meanwhile india has transitioned  from being an occasional buyer to being  a consistent buyer before the war  started india imported well below 5  million barrels of oil from russia on a  monthly basis and many months were even  zero but now india is importing tens of  millions of barrels every month and as  more time passes i think it just becomes  even more likely that more countries are  adopted to policy it's not that they  support the war rather they're simply  gonna do what's in their best interest  and if russia has the cheapest oil then  so be it  aside from western sanctions being  negated by exemptions and neutral  countries the ruble is also  substantially benefiting from supply and  demand while we like to throw around a  bunch of fancy terms like quantitative  easing when describing a country's  currency or economy in the end the  currency strength just comes down to  supply and demand if demand outpaces  apply then the currency will gain  dominance conversely if supply outpaces  demand then the currency will lose  dominance western countries were  obviously trying to accomplish the  latter by eliminating demand but this  has sort of backfired as we just  discussed russia is still able to find  significant amounts of demand for their  oil which in turns means that rubol is  in high demand however the supply of  rubles has gone down substantially  because almost every western company you  can think of has left russia so russians  are no longer giving rubles to foreign  companies if you haven't noticed yet  this means that rubles are entering  russia but not leaving russia russia has  actually leaned into this by  implementing a slew of different rules  for example putin has declared that  importing countries must pay for russian  oil and natural gas using rubles  similarly russian exporters are required  to convert at least half of their excess  revenues into rubles these rules stretch  further than just exports and imports as  well  for instance russia has banned foreign  stock and bondholders from taking their  dividend payments out of russia all of  these policies have just further  inflated the demand for the ruble while  also defining the supply of the ruble  and simple economics would tell you that  this is extremely beneficial for the  ruble not to mention russians themselves  are extremely reluctant to let go of  their rubles because they can earn  extraordinarily high interest rates by  just leaving it in the bank when the war  started russia hiked interest rates all  the way to 20 percent they have since  decreased them to 10  but as long as the currency itself is  holding strong there is very little  reason to seek out investments or spend  your money when you can just earn 10  doing nothing russia has actually  started to lighten such monetary  policies because they don't want the  robot to get too strong you see it if  the rubric continues to get stronger  exports from the country will become  less and less appealing and that's the  last thing russia wants right now so  ironically the ruble is starting to get  so strong that even russia is okay with  a slight cooldown  listening to all of this it might seem  like western sanctions were a massive  fail and from one perspective they were  after all russia is still making loads  of money and the currency is stronger  than ever but it's not all sunshines and  rainbows while russia has plenty of  money to spend it they can't actually  spend it here's the thing while  sanctions may not have been that  effective at stopping countries from  buying russian products they have been  quite successful at preventing russia  from buying foreign products a great  example of this is car sales russia  expects car sales to half by the end of  this year another example is  semiconductors in fact the supply of  semiconductors has gotten so bad in  russia that they have started to use  dishwasher and refrigerator chips inside  military equipment this isn't that  surprising when you consider that u.s  technology exports to russia have  decreased by 70 since the start of the  war this is the same case with  telecommunications lasers avionics and  maritime technology as well and while  russia may be able to put up a strong  facade in the short term it's only a  matter of time until these internal  shortages start destroying not just the  economy but their entire society but  wait a minute doesn't everything get  produced in china why don't chinese  companies just supply russians with  semiconductors and phones and cars not  to mention it they could probably charge  the russians a premium for this right  well they definitely could but none of  the big chinese companies really want to  do this this isn't to say that these  companies support ukraine but at the  same time helping russia isn't in their  best interest either here's the thing  these manufacturing companies have been  selling large contracts with american  juggernauts like apple and nvidia and  the last thing they want to do is  jeopardize their contracts with these  companies by selling equipment to russia  it's simply not worth the risk this  isn't to say that no one is helping  russia though many smaller companies  that don't have relationships with  western countries and companies are  willing to help russia but as you would  guess these are almost always second  grade products so the longer the  sanctions go on the more likely it is  that russia's technology will degrade  aside from facing a supply crisis russia  is also facing a debt crisis recently  russia missed a bond interest payment  not because they couldn't afford it but  because they couldn't pay you see the  interest payments were supposed to be in  euros and dollars but russia doesn't  really have access to these currencies  so they tried paying with rubles but  this payment was blocked by  international sanctions considering this  missing the payment isn't exactly  russia's fault but this will no doubt  discourage investors from buying russian  bonds which will just make it even  harder for russia to get through this  war so while the rule bullet is stronger  than ever the same cannot be said about  the russian economy  in the end russia has played the  sanctions game as well as they can  they've found a new buyers for their oil  they've limited the outflow of rubles  and they've successfully stabilized the  currency all of these efforts have kept  russian income rather consistent but  this isn't to say that russia is winning  the economic war while russia has plenty  of money to spend it they literally  can't spend it they can't buy cars or  phones or computers or really anything  they're forced to rely on chinese  knockoffs that don't have anything to  lose by working with them this hasn't  become that big of a problem yet given  that the sanctions have only been in  place for a few months but imagine what  happens five years down the line what  will everyday russians do when they need  a new car or a new computer russia's  tech industry has already realized this  and that's why tens of thousands of tech  workers have been fleeing the country  and as these tech workers flee the  country it just becomes even less likely  that russia is able to reproduce russian  technology by themselves and in today's  world where technology is basically an  essential russia will only be able to  keep up the trade for so long until  something gives so yes the russian ruble  is indeed the best performing currency  of 2022 but in the end the entire point  of a currency is to act as a medium of  exchange and the given that the  ruleballer is feeling it doing just that  its relative strength is all but  meaningful but